# Push a slate (template + field text) to the Electronic Clapboard over the cone
# bus (physical CAN3), and wait for the panel's own verdict.  Served by
# teensy_bridge_node as clapboard/set_slate — NOT by a node of its own, because
# TeensyLinkClient has exactly one construction site in the package and two
# owners on one UDP socket is a documented bench failure mode.
#
# An ACTION and not a service because the response time is dominated by the
# e-paper, not the wire: a full refresh is 1.5-3.5 s against ~5 ms of CAN
# traffic.  The result must not fire until the clapboard's own CLAP_ACK arrives
# carrying the MEASURED render_ms.
#
# PATCH SEMANTICS.  Only the fields named in field_ids are part of the
# transaction; every other field on the panel retains its previous value.  That
# is what lets a take number be bumped without resending the scene description.
#
# THE DATE IS NOT A FIELD.  The clapboard fills it from its own wall clock,
# slaved to the bridge's 0x7DD time-sync broadcast.  Sending a date here would
# race the Jetson's clock against the one the bridge is already distributing.
#
# THERE IS NO FIRE COMMAND, HERE OR ANYWHERE.  Firing the sync flash is
# button-only by design: "ROS2 gets a complete log of every clap; it does not
# get to clap."
#
# Wire contract: Electronic-Clapboard docs/protocol.md section 8.3/8.4/8.6
# (normative, cross-repository — neither side may change it unilaterally).

# ── Goal ──
uint8 template_id           # Which stored template to composite into (0-15, section 8.4 byte 0)
uint8[] field_ids           # Which fields this transaction sets: 0-7, no duplicates.
                            # Becomes CLAP_COMMIT's field_present_mask.  An EMPTY list is
                            # legal and means "repaint the current content" — useful with
                            # force_full_refresh to clear e-paper ghosting.
string[] field_values       # Parallel to field_ids, same length.  Each at most 32 bytes
                            # when UTF-8 encoded and free of embedded NULs (the receiver
                            # finds the string terminator by scanning for one).  Text is
                            # nominally ASCII; the clapboard renders bytes >= 0x80 as '?'
                            # rather than rejecting the take label, so non-ASCII is
                            # accepted here too.
bool force_full_refresh     # CLAP_COMMIT flags bit0 — force a full panel refresh, which
                            # clears accumulated e-paper ghosting at the cost of a longer
                            # render.
---
# ── Result — the clapboard's terminal verdict, or why it never came ──
bool success                # True iff a CLAP_ACK arrived for THIS txn_id with outcome OK
string outcome              # From the panel (CLAP_ACK section 8.6):
                            #   OK | REJECTED | CRC_MISMATCH | INCOMPLETE | BUSY |
                            #   NO_TEMPLATE | BAD_FIELD_ID | UNKNOWN(n)
                            # Host-side, never a wire value:
                            #   INVALID_GOAL     - malformed, rejected before a frame was built
                            #   DISPATCH_FAILED  - the bridge would not queue the burst
                            #   TIMEOUT          - no CLAP_ACK inside the action's budget
                            # A STRING and not the wire uint8 on purpose: the outcome enum
                            # is the clapboard repo's to allocate, and minting host-only
                            # values inside it would collide the moment they allocate 0x07.
uint16 render_ms            # Panel time MEASURED by the clapboard; 0 when it never rendered
string message              # Human-readable detail: txn_id, and for a non-OK outcome the
                            # bridge's own CLAP_DIAG TX census delta, which is what separates
                            # "the bridge dropped frames" from "the panel mis-reassembled them"
---
# ── Feedback ──
string phase                # SENDING | AWAITING_ACK
                            # There is deliberately no RENDERING phase: the CLAP_SEND RPC
                            # acks the DISPATCH, and the frames then drain onto the bus one
                            # per tick long after it returns, so the host cannot observe the
                            # drain-to-render transition.  The only transition it can see is
                            # the ack, and that ends the goal.
